## 7 — Product demo mechanics & accessibility

The actual dating product isn't connected to a server. Build honest functional demos, not phony backend workflows.

- User selections in the `Taste Matcher` update real local JS state and derived UI. Use a simple deterministic overlap function; don't represent it as validated scientific romance matching.
- The `Join Riff` or `Get the App` CTA must disclose preview status if no working app/store link exists. You may offer an accessible local demo input or pre-composed email link **only if** a real destination is supplied; do not invent a mailbox or tell visitors their email was saved when it wasn't.
- No autoplay copyrighted music. Optional animation equalizer is visual only and labeled so; no fake audio playback claim. If audio previews are added, use only truly licensed/original audio and provide controls/mute.
- Mobile profiles should avoid mandatory face imagery and show taste before portraits. Use accessible alternative text for meaningful collage images; decorative motifs `aria-hidden`.
- Respect user safety/privacy: adult-only positioning, clearly demo data, never request sensitive personal details, do not persist user input without consent.
- Keyboard users can operate dropdowns, tabs, album cards, genre selection, view modes, and the motion toggle. Provide focus rings legible on black, and `aria-live` to announce calculated overlap.
- `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` disables record rotation, parallax, disruptive reveals, and scroll pinning. All features and information remain accessible.

## 8 — Responsive and engineering constraints

### Desktop 1440–1920
- Preserve screenshot's left typographic field (~48%) and right collage (52%). Keep white space around editorial headline and a high-quality sense of physical depth.
- Responsive phones scale together but their actual screens remain legible.

### Tablet
- Allow the collage to rebalance, bringing one phone forward and shifting vinyl to the edges. Headline may wrap across 3–4 carefully controlled lines.

### Mobile 320–430px
- Stack editorial copy before device artwork. Display two phones as an intentional compact layered composition or a user-operated switcher—not a squashed desktop collage.
- No horizontal overflow, missing images, microscopic buttons, inaccessible fixed overlays, or long pinned-scroll traps.
- Scale the texture intensity down; retain red accents, star language, and collage identity with only a few carefully selected pieces.
- Buttons need good tap targets and copy remains readable.

### Performance
- Use transform/opacity and SVG path animation primarily; avoid animating expensive CSS filters on multiple large surfaces.
- Use `IntersectionObserver` for starting/pausing offscreen ambience and `requestAnimationFrame` for time-sensitive loops.
- Lazy-load art below the fold, preload a few hero-critical assets only, compress images to WebP/AVIF or optimized PNG if alpha required.
- Use image `width`/`height`, explicit aspect ratios, and avoid cumulative layout shift.
- If using GSAP/ScrollTrigger, only when substantial choreography benefits; keep graceful fallback if library load fails. No huge dependency graph required.

